White House National Security Advisor  John Bolton 'is shifting president  Trump's plan to quickly withdraw  American troops from Syria Bolton who's  travelling in Israel said yesterday that  the 2,000 troops will not leave Syria  until Isis is defeated and Turkey  guarantees the safety of America's  Kurdish allies he said there's no  timetable for withdrawal last month the  president said it was time for US troops  to come home claiming Isis was defeated  David Martin is at the Pentagon David  good morning good morning president  Trump's plan to immediately withdraw US  troops from Syria was criticized by both  allies and members of his own party and  it triggered the resignation of his  defense secretary James Madison but now  National Security Advisor John Bolton  says the US will not leave Syria until  it accomplishes its mission including  destroying Isis to the point that it  cannot make a comeback the Pentagon says  the US and its allies continue to  conduct air and artillery strikes  against Isis targets in Syria Bolton is  also seeking guarantees from Turkey that  it will not attack Kurdish fighters who  have been allies with the u.s. turkey  considers some Syrian Kurds to be  members of a terrorist group and has  been massing troops on the border to go  after them Bolton is expected to discuss  discuss that issue with Turkish  president air Dewan when they meet in  Agoura tomorrow defense officials were  initially told to have all US troops out  of Syria by the middle of this month but  president Trump acknowledged yesterday  it will take longer and won't happen  quote until Isis is done Gayle thank you  very much David
 

